WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.100
Good morning Hank! It's Tuesday.

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.400
I really liked your video on Friday

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.820
And I thought maybe today we could continue our discussion

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:08.320
of failure by turning to the question of my new book!

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:12.060
So it's been almost five years since my most recent novel The Fault in our Stars was published

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:16.340
Why nothing new since then? Well in my case because a very public success

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.640
happened to coincide with the series of very private failures.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.180
The Fault in our Stars was my fourth novel

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:27.280
but the experience of publishing it was VASTLY different from everything that came before

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:29.340
Like for context in the week after it was published,

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:34.960
The Fault in Our Stars sold more hardcovers than my three previous novels had ever sold, COMBINED!

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.200
Which to be clear was wonderful news!

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.040
But at the same time I was hard at work on this new book about teenage identity thieves

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.840
because you know that's what I did after publishing a book, I wrote a new one!

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.320
But it wasn't working. Now partly the concept itself was flawed. I've always wanted to write a proper thriller,

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:51.580
but I'm not good at writing any of the proper thriller stuff.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:56.220
You know like I'm not great at action scenes or knowing how guns work or or plot!

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:01.360
But also I started to feel this intense pressure, like people were watching over my shoulder as I was writing.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:06.620
By April of 2012 I ditched most of the identity thieves story and re-imagined it as a story about two boys

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:10.180
who find something terrible while exploring storm drains in their little hometown.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:14.020
I wrote about a hundred pages of that over the next few months, but none of it was ever very good though.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:17.860
Meanwhile, The Fault in Our Stars continued to sell and in May of that year I read an amazing screenplay

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:21.600
adaptation of the book, at which point I realized it might actually become a movie!

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:27.180
And somewhere in that period my job stopped being 'person who writes books', which is a present tense job title,

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:31.840
and became 'person who wrote that one book', which is a past tense job title.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:33.880
Like, I remember one night my best friend Chris saying to me

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.900
"It must be weird getting all this money for something you did at Starbucks a couple years ago".

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:42.220
And it was weird. I was elated and grateful that The Fault in Our Stars was reaching so many readers

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:46.120
But at the same time I was terrified because I felt like I could never follow it up!

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:50.620
By the time the movie started shooting in the fall of 2013, I was failing to write a new book

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:55.140
this one about teenage con artists. I spent almost a year on that one, before eventually giving up.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:59.600
I remember being in a hotel room right after the movie came out, looking at these hundreds of pages

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.600
as I realized they would never be a book and just sobbing!

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:07.000
Now I wanna be clear that these are high class problems I am very grateful to have.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.360
But I also wanna be honest about the fact that they are problems.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.380
Writing had always been a safe and sane way for me to pour out my obsessiveness and recursive thinking.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:19.080
But then suddenly it just didn't provide that release anymore and I realized you know, that I... I could STOP.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:23.520
Writing had become kinda miserable and as I devoted more of my time to Crash Course and The Art Assignment

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:27.320
I realized I have amazing work that I am so lucky to do!

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:30.480
I missed writing but in a way you miss someone you used to love.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:34.860
And then about a year ago I went back to it. I started writing something just for fun.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:38.820
I kept saying that to Sarah, "I'm just writing this for fun. I'm just writing this because I think you'll like it".

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:41.900
But of course those turn out to be pretty good reasons to write!

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:45.420
Still though, this is all kinda hard to talk about because the usual arch of these narratives

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:49.700
is I had some failure, but then I plowed through and found greater success than ever!

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:54.520
And I'm not at all sure that's how this story will end. I don't know if I'll ever publish another book.

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:56.820
And if I do, I don't know whether people will like it.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:00.140
I know they probably won't like it as much as they liked The Fault in Our Stars.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:03.500
I hope I finish the book I'm working on. I hope you like it. But I don't know.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:06.580
But I think what changed is that I got okay with not knowing.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:11.380
I used to be so certain. I used to have all these unshakable beliefs about books, and writing, and how to be a writer.

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:13.980
Now my beliefs aren't just unshakable, they're shaken.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.700
That feels uncomfortable, but also productive, I think.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.320
Hank, I got to get back to writing. I'll see you on Friday!

